METH=[('h2', '2.4. Heterogeneity, pleiotropy, outlier and influence diagnostics'), ('para', "Heterogeneity among instruments was assessed with Cochran's Q statistic and the I^^2^^ index for IVW and with Rücker's Q for the MR-Egger framework,{bowden17} with P values above .05 suggesting low heterogeneity. The regression dilution of MR-Egger was assessed with I^^2^^~~GX~~, where values close to 100% indicate that the no-measurement-error (NOME) assumption is reasonable.{bowdenI2} Directional pleiotropy was assessed with the MR-Egger intercept and the MR-PRESSO global test.{verbanck18} Outlying variants were identified with MR-PRESSO (outlier test, 10,000 simulations) and radial MR,{bowden18} and influential variants with Cook's distance (threshold 4/n) and studentized residuals of the IVW regression; estimates were recalculated after removing MR-PRESSO outliers. Leave-one-out analyses determined whether any single variant drove the overall estimate, and funnel plots were inspected for asymmetry. The Steiger filter and the exclusion of the APOE region described above were treated as further sensitivity analyses. Variance explained in the outcome was approximated on the latent logistic scale without a prevalence assumption, which differs slightly from the TwoSampleMR implementation."), ('h2', '2.5. Extended estimators'), ('para', 'To test the consistency of the findings under a wider set of assumptions, we additionally applied maximum likelihood estimation, MR-Lasso,{rees19} the contamination mixture model,{conmix20} the debiased IVW estimator,{ye21} and robust adjusted profile score (MR-RAPS) with Tukey and Huber loss functions and overdispersion,{zhao20} as well as fixed-effect IVW, radial IVW, and IVW restricted to variants with F ≥ 30 or MAF ≥ 0.01, and re-estimated the random-effects IVW with the MendelianRandomization package as a software cross-check. These estimators relax, respectively, the assumptions of a balanced pleiotropy distribution, plurality validity, weak-instrument bias and measurement error in the exposure associations.{slob20} Together with the five methods above, they give 16 estimator variants per dataset, some of which (for example the software cross-check and the MAF restriction, which removed no variants) are re-estimations rather than independent methods.'), ('h2', '2.6. Cross-dataset concordance'), ('para', 'To examine whether the three outcome datasets agree at the level of single variants, we calculated the Wald ratio (outcome effect divided by exposure effect) and its z score for each variant and compared them across datasets by Spearman rank correlation and by the proportion of variants with the same sign. Because the datasets share participants, concordance is expected to be higher than for independent replications and is reported descriptively.'), ('h2', '2.7. Statistical power and sample overlap'), ('para', "We calculated the minimum detectable OR with 80% power at α = .05 from the observed standard error of the IVW estimate in each dataset, and drew power curves across a range of true effects.{brion13,burgess14} Because the exposure GWAS and parts of the outcome data come from the UK Biobank, we considered the bias arising from sample overlap, which for a two-sample design is approximately the overlap proportion divided by the F statistic.{burgess16} We assumed complete overlap for the Fairfield dataset, which gives an upper bound, and note that the same applies in part to the Ghodsian data. Because variants were selected and estimated in the same exposure sample, the F statistic is also subject to winner's curse.{mounier23}"), ('h2', '2.8. Positive control analysis'), ('para', 'To check that the instruments capture a known causal effect, we repeated the analysis with coronary artery disease (CAD) as the outcome, because RC is an established cause of ischaemic heart disease.{varbo13,nord16} We used the 1000 Genomes-based CARDIoGRAMplusC4D meta-analysis (GWAS Catalog GCST003116; 60,801 cases and 123,504 controls; mainly European, South Asian and East Asian ancestry),{nikpay15,sollis23} downloaded in its genome build 37 harmonised form and verified against the published MD5 checksum. The same 52 instruments were used. Instruments missing by rsID were replaced by a variant at the same chromosomal position with the same allele pair (two variants) or by the best proxy with r^^2^^ ≥ 0.8 in the European 1000 Genomes panel (two variants), and two variants without a usable proxy were dropped; one palindromic variant was removed. The analysis was run in Python with the same code as the NAFLD analysis and independently in R with the TwoSampleMR package, in which harmonisation and the five estimators were produced by the package and MR-PRESSO was run with the MRPRESSO package (10,000 simulations, seed 2026). The R analysis was run three times; the first two runs differed from the final run only in logging and in the random seed used for bootstrap standard errors, and all runs are provided. This analysis was added after the NAFLD analysis and was not pre-registered.'), ('h2', '2.9. Software and validation'), ('para', "The NAFLD analyses were conducted with custom Python code implementing the TwoSampleMR and MR-PRESSO algorithms,{hemani18,verbanck18} validated against R output of a previous analysis of the same data (it reproduced the IVW and MR-Egger estimates and standard errors, the Egger intercept, Cochran's Q, the MR-PRESSO global statistic and the outlier-corrected estimate). MR-PRESSO was re-run with the official R package, which identified the same outliers and gave the same corrected estimates as the Python implementation except for a negligible difference in the FinnGen P value (.250 in R against .24 in Python); the values reported here are the R results. Extended estimators were run in R 4.4.3 with the MendelianRandomization (version 0.10.0),{yavorska17} mr.raps (version 0.2) and RadialMR (version 1.2.1) packages. Figures were drawn with matplotlib. All code, console logs and intermediate files are available in the public repository listed under Code availability.")]
EARLIER48='An earlier analysis of these data by our group used a different instrument set whose clumping settings were not recorded; we reconstructed it from its variant list (48 variants, including five in the APOE region) and report it as a sensitivity analysis.'
CHARS='D.table(\'Characteristics of the exposure, outcome and positive-control datasets and of the instruments after harmonisation.\',\n [\'Dataset\',\'Design / source\',\'Cases\',\'Controls\',\'Variants used\',\'R^^2^^ (%)\',\'Mean F (min)\',\'MDE, OR*\'],\n [[\'Exposure: RC\',\'UK Biobank NMR GWAS (Richardson 2022); N = 115,082\',\'–\',\'–\',52,f"{100*S[G][\'R2\']:.1f}",f"{S[G][\'mean_F\']:.0f} ({S[G][\'min_F\']:.1f})",\'–\'],\n  [\'Ghodsian 2021\',\'EHR meta-analysis of 4 cohorts; GCST90091033\',\'8,434\',\'770,180\',48,f"{100*S[G][\'R2\']:.1f}",f"{S[G][\'mean_F\']:.0f} ({S[G][\'min_F\']:.1f})",f"{S[G][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f}"],\n  [\'Fairfield 2022\',\'UK Biobank; GCST90054782\',\'4,761\',\'373,227\',51,f"{100*S[F][\'R2\']:.1f}",f"{S[F][\'mean_F\']:.0f} ({S[F][\'min_F\']:.1f})",f"{S[F][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f}"],\n  [\'FinnGen R5\',\'FinnGen; finn-b-NAFLD\',\'894\',\'217,898\',48,f"{100*S[N][\'R2\']:.1f}",f"{S[N][\'mean_F\']:.0f} ({S[N][\'min_F\']:.1f})",f"{S[N][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f}"],\n  [\'Positive control: CAD\',\'CARDIoGRAMplusC4D 1000G (Nikpay 2015); GCST003116\',\'60,801\',\'123,504\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{100*pcS[\'R2\']:.1f}",f"{pcS[\'mean_F\']:.0f} ({pcS[\'min_F\']:.1f})",f"{pcS[\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f}"]],\n widths=[1.15,2.0,0.55,0.65,0.7,0.5,0.7,0.5],\n note=\'*Minimum detectable odds ratio per 1-SD higher genetically predicted RC with 80% power at α = .05, from the observed standard error of the IVW estimate. EHR = electronic health record; CAD = coronary artery disease; MDE = minimum detectable effect.\')\n'
RES='D.h2(\'3.2. Instrument characteristics and cross-dataset concordance\')\nD.para(f"The 52 independent variants jointly explained {100*S[G][\'R2\']:.1f}% of the variance in RC, with a mean F statistic of {S[G][\'mean_F\']:.0f} (range: {S[G][\'min_F\']:.1f}–{H[G].F.max():.0f}) and an overall F statistic of {S[G][\'F_overall\']:.0f}; every variant had F > 10 and all but none fell below 29 (Fig. S4A). Instrument strength by variant and the single-variant Wald-ratio z scores in the three datasets are shown in Fig. 3. Single-variant Wald ratios were positively correlated across datasets (Spearman ρ = {conc.spearman[0]:.3f} between Ghodsian and Fairfield, P = {pf(conc.p[0])}; {conc.spearman[1]:.3f} between Ghodsian and FinnGen, P = {pf(conc.p[1])}; {conc.spearman[2]:.3f} between Fairfield and FinnGen, P = {pf(conc.p[2])}), and {100*conc.sign_concordance[0]:.0f}%, {100*conc.sign_concordance[1]:.0f}% and {100*conc.sign_concordance[2]:.0f}% of variants had the same sign. Because the datasets share participants and because a few large-effect hepatic variants dominate the pattern, this concordance reflects shared heterogeneity more than independent replication of a causal effect.".replace(\' and all but none fell below 29\',\' and the weakest was 29.8\'))\nD.figure(FIG+\'fig5_variants_heatmap.png\',\'Instrument architecture and concordance across the three NAFLD datasets.\',\'Panel A: effect of each of the 52 variants on remnant cholesterol (SD per allele, RC-increasing orientation). Panel B: z score of the single-variant Wald ratio in each dataset; red indicates that the RC-raising allele is associated with higher NAFLD risk and grey cells indicate variants that were not available. The scale saturates at |z| = 3.\',width=6.2)\nD.h2(\'3.3. Heterogeneity, pleiotropy and outliers\')\nhr=lambda d: het.loc[d]\nD.para(f"Heterogeneity among instruments was substantial in all three datasets (Table 3): Cochran\'s Q was {S[G][\'Q\']:.1f} (47 degrees of freedom; P = {pf(S[G][\'Q_p\'])}) in the Ghodsian data, {S[F][\'Q\']:.1f} (50; P = {pf(S[F][\'Q_p\'])}) in Fairfield and {S[N][\'Q\']:.1f} (47; P = {pf(S[N][\'Q_p\'])}) in FinnGen, corresponding to I^^2^^ values of {100*hr(\'Ghodsian\').I2_IVW:.1f}%, {100*hr(\'Fairfield\').I2_IVW:.1f}% and {100*hr(\'FinnGen\').I2_IVW:.1f}%. The I^^2^^~~GX~~ values were about {100*hr(\'Ghodsian\').I2_GX:.1f}% in each dataset, so regression dilution of MR-Egger was minimal. There was no evidence of directional pleiotropy (MR-Egger intercepts {S[G][\'egger_int\']:.4f}, {S[F][\'egger_int\']:.4f} and {S[N][\'egger_int\']:.4f}; P = {pf(S[G][\'egger_int_p\'])}, {pf(S[F][\'egger_int_p\'])} and {pf(S[N][\'egger_int_p\'])}), although with this much heterogeneity the test has limited power. The MR-PRESSO global test was significant in all three datasets (P < .0001, the smallest value that 10,000 simulations can resolve). MR-PRESSO identified rs112875651 (TRIB1 region) and rs58542926 (TM6SF2) as outliers in every dataset, rs4665972 (near GCKR) in the Ghodsian and Fairfield data, and rs2326077 in the Ghodsian data, and radial MR flagged 8, 7 and 3 variants, a superset of these (Fig. 4, Fig. 5 and Fig. S5). The TM6SF2 variant was the only one with a strong effect in the direction opposite to the rest (Wald ratio about −2.3 log odds of NAFLD per SD in the Ghodsian data), consistent with the known biology of this locus. Cook\'s distance exceeded the 4/n threshold for 2, 5 and 3 variants in the three datasets, and the two most influential variants in every dataset were rs58542926 and rs112875651 (Fig. S4C).")\ntr=[]\nfor ds,o in DS.items():\n    h=hr(ds); rd={\'Ghodsian\':8,\'Fairfield\':7,\'FinnGen\':3}[ds]; ck=int((infl[infl.dataset==ds].cooks_d>infl[infl.dataset==ds].cook_threshold_4_over_n).sum())\n    tr.append([ds,S[o][\'n_used\'],f"{S[o][\'Q\']:.1f} ({pf(S[o][\'Q_p\'])})",f"{100*h.I2_IVW:.1f}",f"{100*h.I2_GX:.1f}",f"{S[o][\'egger_int\']:.4f} ({pf(S[o][\'egger_int_p\'])})",presso.loc[ds].outliers.replace(\'; \',\', \'),rd,ck])\nD.table(\'Heterogeneity, pleiotropy, outlier and influence diagnostics.\',[\'Dataset\',\'SNPs\',\'Q~~IVW~~ (P)\',\'I^^2^^ (%)\',\'I^^2^^~~GX~~ (%)\',\'Egger intercept (P)\',\'MR-PRESSO outliers\',\'Radial outliers\',\'Cook > 4/n\'],tr,widths=[0.7,0.5,1.05,0.45,0.55,0.95,1.5,0.55,0.55],size=8,note=\'The MR-PRESSO global test had P < .0001 in all three datasets. Q~~IVW~~ = Cochran’s Q for the IVW estimate; I^^2^^ = proportion of variability due to heterogeneity; I^^2^^~~GX~~ = instrument-strength measure for MR-Egger; PRESSO = pleiotropy residual sum and outlier test (R package).\')\nD.figure(FIG+\'fig3_scatter_all.png\',\'Variant-level associations with remnant cholesterol and NAFLD in the three datasets.\',\'Alleles are aligned to the RC-raising allele. Grey bars are 95% confidence intervals, the solid line is the IVW estimate, the dashed line is the MR-Egger fit and the dotted line is the weighted median. Circled points are MR-PRESSO outliers; the TM6SF2 variant lies far below the regression line in all three datasets.\',width=6.7)\nD.figure(FIG+\'fig4_diagnostics_Ghodsian.png\',\'Diagnostics for the causal effect of remnant cholesterol on NAFLD in the Ghodsian meta-analysis.\',\'Panel A: scatter plot of variant effects on remnant cholesterol and on NAFLD with the IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median and weighted mode fits; the near-zero slopes of all methods indicate no association. Panel B: funnel plot of single-variant Wald ratios against precision; the asymmetric scatter outside the funnel reflects the heterogeneity driven by a few hepatic loci. Panel C: leave-one-out analysis, showing that no single variant changes the estimate materially. Panel D: forest plot of the single-variant Wald ratios. The equivalent figures for the Fairfield and FinnGen datasets are in Supplemental Digital Content 2 (Figs. S2 and S3).\',width=6.0)\nD.h2(\'3.4. Sensitivity analyses\')\nlo_g,hi_g,_=loo(G); lo_f,hi_f,_=loo(F); lo_n,hi_n,_=loo(N)\npg,pfa,pn=pr(\'Ghodsian\'),pr(\'Fairfield\'),pr(\'FinnGen\')\nD.para(f"The estimates were stable across the planned sensitivity analyses (Table 4). After removal of MR-PRESSO outliers, the IVW estimate remained null in the Ghodsian (OR = {pg[\'OR\']:.3f}, 95% CI: {pg[\'lo\']:.3f}–{pg[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(pg[\'p\'])}; {pg[\'n\']} variants), Fairfield (OR = {pfa[\'OR\']:.3f}, 95% CI: {pfa[\'lo\']:.3f}–{pfa[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(pfa[\'p\'])}; {pfa[\'n\']} variants) and FinnGen data (OR = {pn[\'OR\']:.3f}, 95% CI: {pn[\'lo\']:.3f}–{pn[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(pn[\'p\'])}; {pn[\'n\']} variants), with much narrower intervals in the first two. The Steiger filter removed 3, 4 and 13 variants in the three datasets without changing the conclusion; the 13 removed in FinnGen probably reflect the small number of cases (894), for which variance explained in the outcome is estimated with little precision, so this result should be read with caution. No leave-one-out estimate reached P < .05 (OR ranges {lo_g:.3f}–{hi_g:.3f}, {lo_f:.3f}–{hi_f:.3f} and {lo_n:.3f}–{hi_n:.3f}; Fig. 5C and Figs. S2–S3). Excluding the APOE region, restricting to variants with F ≥ 30 and excluding variants with MAF < 0.01 (which removed none) did not materially alter the effect estimates or their precision. The reconstructed earlier instrument set gave IVW odds ratios of {g(G,df=o48)[\'OR\']:.3f} (95% CI: {g(G,df=o48)[\'lo\']:.3f}–{g(G,df=o48)[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(g(G,df=o48)[\'p\'])}; 41 variants), {g(F,df=o48)[\'OR\']:.3f} (95% CI: {g(F,df=o48)[\'lo\']:.3f}–{g(F,df=o48)[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(g(F,df=o48)[\'p\'])}; 48 variants) and {g(N,df=o48)[\'OR\']:.3f} (95% CI: {g(N,df=o48)[\'lo\']:.3f}–{g(N,df=o48)[\'hi\']:.3f}, P = {pf(g(N,df=o48)[\'p\'])}; 43 variants), with much less heterogeneity (Cochran\'s Q P = {pf(S48[G][\'Q_p\'])}, {pf(S48[F][\'Q_p\'])} and {pf(S48[N][\'Q_p\'])}). None of the three estimates is significant. We report this analysis in full because the change of instrument set affects the heterogeneity and, in FinnGen, the point estimate, which falls in the protective direction.")\nsr=[]\nfor lab,fn in [(\'All variants\',lambda o:g(o)),(\'MR-PRESSO outliers removed\',lambda o:pr(NAME[o])),(\'Steiger-filtered\',lambda o:g(o,an=\'Steiger-filtered\')),(\'APOE region excluded\',lambda o:g(o,df=apoe)),(\'F ≥ 30 only\',lambda o:ex(NAME[o],\'IVW, variants with F>=30\')),(\'Earlier 48-variant set\',lambda o:g(o,df=o48))]:\n    row=[lab]\n    for o in (G,F,N):\n        r=fn(o); row.append(f"{r[\'OR\']:.3f} ({r[\'lo\']:.3f}–{r[\'hi\']:.3f}), {pf(r[\'p\'])} [{r[\'n\']}]")\n    sr.append(row)\nD.table(\'Inverse variance weighted estimates in sensitivity analyses.\',[\'Analysis\',\'Ghodsian: OR (95% CI), P\',\'Fairfield: OR (95% CI), P\',\'FinnGen: OR (95% CI), P\'],sr,widths=[1.4,1.8,1.8,1.8],note=\'MR-PRESSO-corrected estimates are from the R package. Square brackets give the number of variants. The earlier 48-variant set is described in section 2.2.\')\nD.h2(\'3.5. Extended robust estimators\')\nD.para(f"Eleven further estimators, which relax different assumptions about the instruments, gave results that were uniformly compatible with the null (Table 5 and Fig. 2). In the Ghodsian data the odds ratios ranged from {ext[ext.dataset==\'Ghodsian\'].OR.min():.3f} to {ext[ext.dataset==\'Ghodsian\'].OR.max():.3f}, in the Fairfield data from {ext[ext.dataset==\'Fairfield\'].OR.min():.3f} to {ext[ext.dataset==\'Fairfield\'].OR.max():.3f} and in FinnGen from {ext[ext.dataset==\'FinnGen\'].OR.min():.3f} to {ext[ext.dataset==\'FinnGen\'].OR.max():.3f}. MR-Lasso retained 40, 45 and 46 variants as valid and the contamination mixture model classified 33, 41 and 38 variants as valid, and both gave estimates close to the IVW result; MR-RAPS with robust loss functions and the debiased IVW estimator, which down-weight outliers and correct for weak-instrument bias, did the same. The fixed-effect IVW intervals were narrower than the random-effects intervals because they ignore the heterogeneity, and they are reported for completeness only.")\ner=[]\nfor m,lab in [(\'Maximum likelihood\',\'Maximum likelihood\'),(\'MR-Lasso\',\'MR-Lasso\'),(\'Contamination mixture\',\'Contamination mixture\'),(\'Debiased IVW\',\'Debiased IVW\'),(\'MR-RAPS (tukey, overdispersed)\',\'MR-RAPS, Tukey\'),(\'MR-RAPS (huber, overdispersed)\',\'MR-RAPS, Huber\'),(\'IVW (fixed effect)\',\'IVW, fixed effect\'),(\'Radial IVW (modified second order weights)\',\'Radial IVW\')]:\n    row=[lab]\n    for ds in (\'Ghodsian\',\'Fairfield\',\'FinnGen\'):\n        r=ex(ds,m); row.append(f"{r[\'OR\']:.3f} ({r[\'lo\']:.3f}–{r[\'hi\']:.3f})")\n    er.append(row)\nD.table(\'Odds ratio of NAFLD per 1-SD higher genetically predicted remnant cholesterol from extended estimators.\',[\'Estimator\',\'Ghodsian: OR (95% CI)\',\'Fairfield: OR (95% CI)\',\'FinnGen: OR (95% CI)\'],er,widths=[1.9,1.7,1.7,1.7],note=\'MR-RAPS = robust adjusted profile score; all analyses use the primary 52-variant set after harmonisation (48, 51 and 48 variants). IVW with F ≥ 30 and with MAF ≥ 0.01 are in Table 4 and Supplemental Digital Content 2.\')\nD.h2(\'3.6. Statistical power and sample overlap\')\nD.para(f"With 80% power at α = .05, the minimum detectable OR per 1-SD was {S[G][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f} in the Ghodsian data, {S[F][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f} in Fairfield and {S[N][\'mde_or_80pct_power\']:.2f} in FinnGen (or {S[G][\'mde_or_80pct_power_protective\']:.2f}, {S[F][\'mde_or_80pct_power_protective\']:.2f} and {S[N][\'mde_or_80pct_power_protective\']:.2f} for a protective effect; Fig. 6). The confidence intervals therefore exclude a large harmful effect in the two larger datasets (upper limits {gi[\'hi\']:.2f} and {fi[\'hi\']:.2f}) but cannot exclude a small to moderate one, and the FinnGen result is compatible with a wide range of effects. With an overall F statistic of about {S[G][\'F_overall\']:.0f}, bias from complete sample overlap would be at most about {100*S[G][\'overlap_relative_bias_if_full\']:.1f}% of the confounded association, although this figure is optimistic because of winner\'s curse. These power calculations are post hoc, assume valid instruments and refer to a lifelong difference of 1 SD in RC.")\nD.figure(FIG+\'fig6_power.png\',\'Statistical power and precision of the three outcome datasets.\',\'Panel A: power to detect a given true odds ratio per 1-SD higher remnant cholesterol, computed from the observed IVW standard errors; the dashed line marks 80% power. Panel B: IVW odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals against the number of cases (log scale), showing how precision rises with case numbers.\',width=6.4)\nD.h2(\'3.7. Positive control: coronary artery disease\')\niv=pcR.iloc[0]; eg=pcR.iloc[1]; wm=pcR.iloc[2]; sm=pcR.iloc[3]; wmo=pcR.iloc[4]\npc_pr=pcP[pcP[\'MR Analysis\']==\'Outlier-corrected\'].iloc[0]\nD.para(f"Forty-nine of the 52 instruments were available for CAD (46 found directly by rsID, two by position and two by proxy; two were absent without a proxy and one ambiguous palindromic variant was removed). They explained {100*pcS[\'R2\']:.1f}% of the variance in RC (mean F = {pcS[\'mean_F\']:.0f}). In contrast to the NAFLD results, genetically predicted RC was strongly associated with CAD (IVW OR = {iv.OR:.3f}, 95% CI: {iv.OR_lo:.3f}–{iv.OR_hi:.3f}, P = {pf(iv.pval)}; Table 6 and Fig. 7). The weighted median ({wm.OR:.3f}), MR-Egger ({eg.OR:.3f}) and mode-based estimates ({sm.OR:.3f} and {wmo.OR:.3f}) pointed in the same direction. Heterogeneity was large (Q = {pcS[\'Q\']:.1f}, 48 degrees of freedom, P = {pf(pcS[\'Q_p\'])}), but the MR-Egger intercept gave no evidence of directional pleiotropy ({pcE.egger_intercept[0]:.4f}, P = {pf(pcE.pval[0])}). The association remained after removal of MR-PRESSO outliers (R: six outliers, OR = {np.exp(pc_pr[\'Causal Estimate\']):.3f}; Python: five outliers, OR = {pcS[\'presso_corr_or\']:.3f}), after the Steiger filter (OR = {pcS[\'steiger_ivw_or\']:.3f}; one variant removed) and in the leave-one-out analysis (ORs 1.51–1.62, all P < 10^^−8^^). The Python and R analyses gave identical IVW, MR-Egger and weighted median estimates and differed slightly only for the weighted mode and the number of MR-PRESSO outliers, as in the NAFLD analysis. The instruments therefore reproduce the known association of RC with coronary disease, which supports their relevance and their ability to detect an effect on a clinical outcome in these data, although it does not exclude pleiotropy specific to liver fat.")\npr_rows=[[\'IVW\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{iv.OR:.3f}",f"{iv.OR_lo:.3f}–{iv.OR_hi:.3f}",pf(iv.pval)],[\'MR-Egger\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{eg.OR:.3f}",f"{eg.OR_lo:.3f}–{eg.OR_hi:.3f}",pf(eg.pval)],[\'Weighted median\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{wm.OR:.3f}",f"{wm.OR_lo:.3f}–{wm.OR_hi:.3f}",pf(wm.pval)],[\'Simple mode\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{sm.OR:.3f}",f"{sm.OR_lo:.3f}–{sm.OR_hi:.3f}",pf(sm.pval)],[\'Weighted mode\',pcS[\'n_used\'],f"{wmo.OR:.3f}",f"{wmo.OR_lo:.3f}–{wmo.OR_hi:.3f}",pf(wmo.pval)],[\'IVW after MR-PRESSO outlier removal (R)\',43,f"{np.exp(pc_pr[\'Causal Estimate\']):.3f}",f"{np.exp(pc_pr[\'Causal Estimate\']-1.96*pc_pr[\'Sd\']):.3f}–{np.exp(pc_pr[\'Causal Estimate\']+1.96*pc_pr[\'Sd\']):.3f}",pf(pc_pr[\'P-value\'])]]\nD.table(\'Positive control: Mendelian randomization estimates of coronary artery disease per 1-SD higher genetically predicted remnant cholesterol.\',[\'Method\',\'SNPs\',\'OR\',\'95% CI\',\'P\'],pr_rows,widths=[2.6,0.6,0.8,1.3,1.0],note=\'R (TwoSampleMR 0.6.22 and MRPRESSO); Nikpay et al 2015. The Python analysis gave identical IVW, MR-Egger and weighted median estimates (Supplemental Digital Content 2, Table S7).\')\nD.figure(FIG+\'fig7_positive_control_contrast.png\',\'The same 52 instruments give a clear signal for coronary artery disease and none for NAFLD.\',\'Odds ratios per 1-SD higher genetically predicted remnant cholesterol for the positive control (coronary artery disease, purple) and for NAFLD in the three datasets. The IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median and mode-based estimates are shown for each outcome.\',width=6.0)\n'
